cystic fibrosis is a hereditary disease  that affects thousands of people each  year  it causes mucus to build up in the lungs  leaving affected patients more  vulnerable to infection than healthy  people patients like Gary are thankful  for medications containing the  aminoglycoside tobramycin they help to  combat his respiratory system infections  caused by the bacteria of Pseudomonas  aeruginosa  however the misuse of these medications  can make them less effective gary was  prescribed toby an inhaled medication  containing tobramycin gary only takes  toby when he is feeling sick not  according to his prescription let's take  a closer look at how this affects Toby's  effectiveness here are 3 Pseudomonas  aeruginosa each one is represented as a  little house and here's Toby and his  molecular form toby helps Gary eliminate  the bacteria by attacking the rapa somes  normal ribosomes have two subunits and  create protein through translation a  small 30s subunit binds to a strand of  mRNA routing it to the larger 50s  subunit the larger subunit fixes the  complementary tRNAs and they're attached  amino acids to create a polypeptide  chain  Toby binds to the 30s subunit  interrupting translation the resulting  protein is either terminated early or  not proof-read without the correct  proteins the bacteria eventually die  sometimes toby isn't able to do his job  some bacteria produce enzymes that  modify amino glycosides by acetylation  phosphorylation or a generation this  prevents Toby from binding to the  ribosomes and inhibiting protein  production these resistant bacteria are  able to reproduce and create new  generations of resistant bacteria making  Toby useless don't be like Garry finish  your antibiotics use them according to  your prescriptions and don't use them  when you don't have to for more  information about how to combat  antibiotic resistance and stay healthy  visit the PDB website  [Music]
 

